      "latency_ms": 45058.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1236,
      "output": "At its most basic level, water is a chemical compound with the formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\n*   **Composition:** It consists of two parts hydrogen and one part oxygen.\n*   **Polarity:** The oxygen atom is slightly more \"electronegative\" (it pulls electrons toward itself) than the hydrogen atoms. This gives water a **polar** molecular structure. The oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion).\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties\nWater behaves differently from most other liquids due to its molecular structure:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. It is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can carry nutrients, minerals, and waste products throughout the body and the environment.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots against gravity.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the temperature of the Earth and keeps the human body from overheating.\n\n### 3. States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, the molecules lock into a rigid crystal lattice.\n*   **Liquid:** The most common state, where molecules can move past one another.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, the molecules move so fast they escape the liquid surface.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is essential for life as we know it. It is the primary component of all living things (humans are about 60% water). It is used by cells for:\n*   **Transport:** Moving nutrients and oxygen to cells.\n*   **Chemical Reactions:** Facilitating the chemical reactions needed for digestion and energy production.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** Sweating and panting to cool the body down."
